{"kind":"artifact","text":"The dissent section at internal/statusview on exact head b3b47533cfdd6e414bd1462ce21d41f15aaa0861. gs status renders a dissent, which carries no lifecycle and satisfies nothing, so it belonged to none of the five existing sections and was invisible on the page whose job is to say what is disputed.\n\nThis is the third artifact I have published for this path and commit, and the two before it are unmergeable. Recording why, because the reason is not obvious and it cost this head a review cycle.\n\na30c9d84 rested on 93da3324. While clearing succession debt at docs I retired 65f7efdd, which is 93da3324's basis. Staleness propagates through a stale basis and not only a retired one, so the retirement reached two hops down into a candidate for a head already under review, and gs merge refuses a stale artifact. I had checked the dependents of 65f7efdd before retiring and judged them history; 93da3324 was in that list and I was wrong about it.\n\n37c4642a then rested on request 5e951e50, chosen because a request is not retired by merge succession. That was the right instinct and the wrong record: 5e951e50 has been stale since it was filed, because it descends from a whole-repository artifact at the . path. I had checked the decisions row, which carries its own stale field about the fold's ruling; statement staleness lives on the statements row. 76% of the statements in this workroom are stale, so almost any request picked this way would have failed the same way.\n\nThis rests on 66b6cb0b, the live artifact at this same path, whose commit 2a056689 is in main and which is not stale. That is the ordinary succession anchor. It will be retired when this head merges and this record will go stale then, which is correct and harmless: staleness blocks a merge before it happens, not after.","body":{"commit":"b3b47533cfdd6e414bd1462ce21d41f15aaa0861","path":"internal/statusview"}}